  "routing": {
    "_comment": "tier1_triad_bonus: points added to the triad section when a tier-1 entity (Sentinel, Columbia, Trident, W87, Pantex, KCNSC...) appears. Program and site names should beat generic event language like 'milestone b' or 'contract award'. Lower it if triad starts absorbing stories that merely mention a program in passing.",
    "tier1_triad_bonus": 8
  },
  "semantic": {
    "_comment": "Model-based relevance for the ambiguous middle. Keyword rules cannot tell a land-based-deterrence essay from a Nicaragua politics essay, or an AI-silicon story from an AI-paperwork story. Only items scoring between floor and ceiling are sent: high scorers already have hard evidence (dollars, corroboration, tier-1 programme), low scorers are noise. Set enabled=false to disable entirely. Without ANTHROPIC_API_KEY it silently no-ops.",
    "enabled": true,
    "floor": -5.0,
    "ceiling": 26.0,
    "batch_size": 25,
    "max_items": 200,
    "deltas": {
      "0": -28,
      "1": -12,
      "2": 2,
      "3": 8
    },
    "thesis": "You are filtering a news brief for a private-equity investor whose thesis is the DEFENSE INDUSTRIAL BASE - specifically sub-tier suppliers (Tier 2-4) of engineered components, specialty processing, and accredited testing, with a focus on the US nuclear enterprise (Sentinel, Columbia/Trident, B-21, LRSO, warheads, NNSA sites such as Pantex, Y-12 and Kansas City), missiles and energetics, specialty materials (carbon phenolic, ablatives, solid rocket motors, castings, forgings), rad-hard microelectronics, and commercial/installation nuclear power as a secondary market.\n\nWhat matters: qualification barriers, sole-source positions, production capacity and bottlenecks, supplier-base health, contract awards that signal demand, M&A comps, and the hardware demand that AI and autonomy pull through (compute, sensors, power, packaging, thermal).\n\nWhat does NOT matter: geopolitics and foreign policy commentary, personnel moves, ceremonies, software and IT applications with no hardware implication, general military operations, and allied political relations.\n\nCalibration notes:\n- Threat and capability assessments of peer adversaries (Chinese or Russian modernisation, Taiwan contingency, Pacific posture, munitions consumption rates in Ukraine) are relevance 2: they are the demand driver behind procurement volumes, stockpile targets and capacity investment. Rate them 3 when they draw explicit conclusions about force structure, industrial capacity or weapons inventories.\n- Regional and domestic politics of other countries with no bearing on US force structure or procurement (leadership succession, internal reform, bilateral diplomacy) are relevance 0.\n- Cyber, IT, networks and software with no hardware procurement implication are relevance 1.\n- Anything about schools, education, healthcare, personnel policy, facilities management, chemical or border security, or disaster response is relevance 0 regardless of which agency published it. GAO in particular audits the entire federal government.\n- Nuclear enterprise oversight and DOE contracting ARE relevant (relevance 2-3) because they bear on how the weapons complex buys from suppliers.",
    "section_guide": {
      "triad": "US nuclear enterprise: Sentinel/ICBM, Columbia/Trident/SLBM, B-21, LRSO, SLCM-N, warheads, NNSA sites (Pantex, Y-12, Kansas City, Los Alamos, Livermore, Savannah River). News about these programmes goes here even if it mentions a contract or budget.",
      "dib": "Industrial base mechanics: supplier capacity, bottlenecks, sole/second sourcing, qualification, energetics and specialty materials, castings and forgings, shipyards and depots, skilled labour, critical minerals, DPA Title III.",
      "contracts": "Specific contract awards, task orders, IDIQs, production decisions and competitions - the transaction itself.",
      "budget": "Appropriations, NDAA, reprogramming, acquisition policy and reform, GAO oversight of defence spending, programme cost and schedule baselines.",
      "primes": "Corporate news about major contractors: earnings, guidance, backlog, restructuring, layoffs, facilities, executives.",
      "deals": "M&A, private equity, take-privates, divestitures, funding rounds, IPOs, antitrust - ownership and capital transactions.",
      "nuclear_energy": "Commercial and installation nuclear power: reactors, restarts, SMRs and microreactors, fuel cycle, utility and hyperscaler deals, DOE loans.",
      "tech": "Enabling hardware and the systems that pull it: rad-hard microelectronics, trusted foundry, packaging, sensors, power, autonomy, unmanned, directed energy, space. Cyber and software items belong here too, at low relevance.",
      "global": "Foreign and allied defence: other nations' procurement, FMS and exports, alliance politics, foreign conflicts.",
      "thinktank": "ONLY genuine commentary, essays and research reports that argue a position or analyse a question. Never use this for news reporting, government announcements or contract news - those belong in a substantive section even if the fit is imperfect."
    }
  }
}